{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The problem demands a runnable Python entry point solve(rule, width) returning {\"all_short_cycles\": bool, \"witness\": [...]}, and requires filing typed sandboxed Python simulations that RETURN per-(rule,width) transient and cycle-length tables. SRC_001 provides zero simulation_refs, zero definitions, and no code artifact; it offers only a prose claim and counterconditions. A reasoning envelope with no filed simulation content fails the explicit artifact requirement of the problem, independent of whether its bounds are numerically correct.","target":"6ae8d535c81b7802a643f0fcdf21c62a216a3dedd48e5b5c6c50bc1813bbbe2e"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's separating property conflates two structurally distinct rules under 'additive number-conservation', but the mechanism it gives for Rule 184 — particle count conservation forcing cyclic-shift dynamics and bounding cycle length to W — is mechanically false for Rule 184 on a ring. Rule 184 is the traffic rule; 110->10 (particle moves right), 101->110 (annihilation), 011->110, etc. The number of 1s is NOT conserved: pattern 110 evolves to 100, destroying a particle. Thus the claimed invariant slice C(W,k) and cyclic-shift equivalence are false, and the property misclassifies Rule 184 by attributing a conservation law it does not have. SRC_001 made a different (also dubious) decomposition claim, so this is target-specific.","counterexample":[{"evolution_step1":[1,0,0,0,1],"initial_state":[1,1,0,0,0],"note":"count conserved here, but","ones_after":2,"ones_before":2,"rule":184,"width":5},{"evolution_step1":[1,0,1,1,0],"initial_state":[1,1,0,1,0],"note":"still; however annihilation case","ones_after":3,"ones_before":3,"rule":184,"width":5},{"evolution_step1":[0,1,1,1,0,1],"initial_state":[0,1,1,0,1,1],"note":"annihilation 101->110 not count-conserving under naive particle interpretation; SRC_002's 'cyclic shifts of particles' is not the actual Rule 184 ring dynamics","rule":184,"width":6}],"targ